NEW_SECTION
    var self = this,cssWidth = "",exitOnAway=true,currentForm = null,id = null,effectName = "none",bodyOldPosition = "",mainFormCon = "",mainFormConInner = "",overlayBackgroundType = "color",overlayStyle= "hsla(0, 0%, 100%, 0.48)",initialized = false,openProcessor = function() {},closeProcessor = function() {},modalOn = false,sY = 0,computedModalHeight = 0,computedModalWidth = 0;
    var defaultModalWidths = ["500px", "500px", "86%"],modalWidths = defaultModalWidths, brkpoints = { largeStart: 1000, mediumStart: 520 },pageContainer = null,className = "",formIdAttribute = "",closeButtonClass = "",modalWidthsAttribute = "";

REASON
Before modification the closure declared ten variables that were assigned but never
read, or never assigned at all: totalHeight, closeButton, sX, endSy, scrollable,
modalHeigthBelow, modalHeigthAbove and paddingTop. They were leftovers of the
pre-v4 positioning workflow and only obscured the module's real state. They are
removed. `defaultModalWidths` is added to hold the responsive default triple so
that a per-trigger width override can be reverted (see CHANGE 6).

================================================================================
CHANGE 2 — Fire openProcessor for the "none" effect
================================================================================

OLD_SECTION
            modal.innerHTML = "";

            modayBody.classList.add("show");
        },
        split: function(modal) {

NEW_SECTION
            modal.innerHTML = "";

            modayBody.classList.add("show");
            openProcessor();
        },
        split: function(modal) {

REASON
`openProcessor` is documented as the "modal is now open" callback, but it was only
invoked from the `transitionend` handler, which the three animated effects reach and
the `none` effect never does. With `effect = "none"` (the default) a consumer's
open callback therefore never ran. Since the `none` effect is synchronous, the call
is made directly after the overlay is shown. Verified by the
"[none] openProcessor called" check.

================================================================================
CHANGE 3 — Rewrite positionVertically(); remove undefined VerticalScroll reference
================================================================================

OLD_SECTION
    function positionVertically(modal, height) {
        var browserHeight = window.innerHeight;
        var diff = browserHeight - height;
        sX = window.scrollX;
        sY = window.scrollY;
        modalHeigthBelow = ((paddingTop * 2) + computedModalHeight) - window.innerHeight;
        if (diff < 100) {
            scrollable = true;
            var heightBelow = VerticalScroll.query(parseInt($$.sm("html").cssStyle("height"), "px"))["remainingHeightBelow"];
            if (heightBelow >= modalHeigthBelow) {
                modal.style["top"] = "50px";
                modal.style["padding-bottom"] = "50px";
                modal.style["transform"] = "translateY(0%) translateX(-50%)";
            } else {
                modalHeigthAbove = ((paddingTop * 2) + computedModalHeight) - window.innerHeight;
                if (modalHeigthAbove > 0) {
                    modal.style["top"] = "50" + "px";
                    modal.style["padding-bottom"] = "50px";
                    modal.style["transform"] = "translateY(0%) translateX(-50%)";
                } else {
                    $$.sm(modal).center();
                }
            }
        }
        if (modalOn == true) {

NEW_SECTION
    function positionVertically(modal, height) {
        var browserHeight = window.innerHeight;
        var diff = browserHeight - height;
        sY = window.scrollY;
        if (diff < 100) { //modal (nearly) taller than viewport: pin to top and let the overlay scroll
            modal.style["top"] = "50px";
            modal.style["padding-bottom"] = "50px";
            modal.style["transform"] = "translateY(0%) translateX(-50%)";
        }
        if (modalOn == true) {

REASON
This was the module's most serious defect. `VerticalScroll` is not defined anywhere
in the library (confirmed by grepping the whole repository — the only occurrence was
this call site), so every attempt to open a modal within 100px of the viewport
height threw `ReferenceError: VerticalScroll is not defined`, aborting the open
half-way and leaving the overlay in a broken state that also broke the next
`close()` call. Tall modals were completely unusable.

Inspecting the dead branches shows they were redundant regardless: both the `then`
and the `else > if` branch applied exactly the same three style declarations, and
`modalHeigthAbove` was computed with the identical expression as `modalHeigthBelow`.
So the whole `VerticalScroll` query only ever chose between two identical outcomes.
The remaining `else > else` branch (`$$.sm(modal).center()`) was unreachable in
practice: it required `modalHeigthAbove <= 0`, i.e. a modal shorter than
`innerHeight - 100`, which contradicts the enclosing `diff < 100` condition.

The function is therefore reduced to its one meaningful behaviour: when the modal
is at least as tall as the viewport minus 100px, pin it 50px from the top with
bottom padding; otherwise leave the CSS-driven vertical centring in place. This
removes the crash and preserves the intended layout. Paired with CHANGE 8, which
makes the overlay scrollable so a pinned tall modal is actually reachable.

================================================================================
CHANGE 4 — Extract restoreScrollPosition(); stop forcing smooth scroll on <html>
================================================================================

OLD_SECTION
        mainFormCon = "";
        mainFormConInner = "";
        bodyOldPosition = "";
        $$.ss("html").style["scroll-behavior"] = "unset";
        scrollTo(0, sY);
        closeProcessor();
        if (overlayBackgroundType == "blur" && pageContainer != null) pageContainer.classList.remove("vxKit");
        $$.ss("html").style["scroll-behavior"] = "smooth";
    }

NEW_SECTION
        mainFormCon = "";
        mainFormConInner = "";
        bodyOldPosition = "";
        restoreScrollPosition();
        closeProcessor();
        if (overlayBackgroundType == "blur" && pageContainer != null) pageContainer.classList.remove("vxKit");
    }

    function restoreScrollPosition() { //jump back instantly, then restore the page's own scroll-behavior
        var htmlEle = $$.ss("html");
        var previousScrollBehavior = htmlEle.style["scroll-behavior"];
        htmlEle.style["scroll-behavior"] = "auto";
        scrollTo(0, sY);
        htmlEle.style["scroll-behavior"] = previousScrollBehavior;
    }

REASON
Closing a modal permanently rewrote the host page's `scroll-behavior` to `smooth`
as an inline style on `<html>`. That is a global side effect on the consumer's page
that outlives the modal, silently changing the behaviour of every subsequent anchor
jump and `scrollTo()` on the site, and it was never undone. The new helper suppresses
smooth scrolling only for the duration of the restore jump (so the position snaps
back rather than animating) and then puts the page's own inline value back, whatever
it was. The logic is extracted into a function because the same restore is now needed
from two call sites (see CHANGE 5).

