CHANGE 5 — Make the "none" close path consistent with the animated ones
================================================================================

OLD_SECTION
            modalCon.removeChild(recallCurrent);
            modalBody.classList.remove("show");
            modalOn = false;
            scrollable = false;
            document.body.style["position"] = bodyOldPosition;
            document.body.style["top"] = "0";
            scrollTo(0, sY);
            closeProcessor();
        },

NEW_SECTION
            modalCon.removeChild(recallCurrent);
            modalBody.classList.remove("show");
            modalOn = false;
            document.body.style["position"] = bodyOldPosition;
            document.body.style["top"] = "0";
            restoreScrollPosition();
            closeProcessor();
            if (overlayBackgroundType == "blur" && pageContainer != null) pageContainer.classList.remove("vxKit");
        },

REASON
Two inconsistencies between the `none` close path and `releaseModal()` (used by the
animated effects). First, the blur overlay was never taken down: with
`overlayBackgroundType = "blur"` and the default `none` effect, `show()` added the
`vxKit` blur class to `pageContainer` but nothing removed it, so the page stayed
permanently blurred after the modal closed. The removal line is added. Second, the
raw `scrollTo` is routed through `restoreScrollPosition()` so both paths handle
`scroll-behavior` identically. `scrollable = false` drops with its variable.

================================================================================
CHANGE 6 — Delegate trigger matching with closest(); reset per-trigger widths
================================================================================

OLD_SECTION
            if (closeButtonClass != "") {
                if (e.target.classList.contains(closeButtonClass)) {
                    self.close();
                }
            }
            if (e.target.classList.contains(className)) {

                var formId = e.target.getAttribute(formIdAttribute);
                var form = document.getElementById(formId);
                if (form != null) {
                    if(e.target.getAttribute(modalWidthsAttribute) != null){
                        modalWidths = e.target.getAttribute(modalWidthsAttribute).split(",");
                    }
                    show(form);
                }
            }

NEW_SECTION
            if (closeButtonClass != "") {
                if (e.target.closest("." + closeButtonClass) != null) {
                    self.close();
                }
            }
            var trigger = e.target.closest("." + className);
            if (trigger != null) {
                var formId = trigger.getAttribute(formIdAttribute);
                var form = document.getElementById(formId);
                if (form != null) {
                    if(trigger.getAttribute(modalWidthsAttribute) != null){
                        modalWidths = trigger.getAttribute(modalWidthsAttribute).split(",");
                    }else{
                        modalWidths = defaultModalWidths;
                    }
                    show(form);
                }
            }

REASON
Two problems. (a) `e.target.classList.contains(...)` only matched when the user
clicked the trigger element itself. A trigger containing markup — an icon, a
`<span>` label, anything nested — delivered the child as `e.target`, so the click was
ignored. Nested content in triggers and close buttons is ordinary usage, so both
checks now walk up with `closest()`.
(b) `modalWidths` was overwritten whenever a trigger carried the widths attribute
but was never restored, so a single trigger with custom widths permanently changed
the sizing of every other modal opened afterwards for the life of the instance.
A trigger without the attribute now explicitly restores `defaultModalWidths`.
Verified by the "data-widths attribute honoured" and "default widths restored on
next open" checks.

================================================================================
CHANGE 7 — Fix overlay dedupe selector and overlay background application
================================================================================

OLD_SECTION
        if ($$.ss("style[data-id='vModalStyles']") == null) {
            //Create
            var overlay = $$.ce("DIV");
            var effectsCon = $$.ce("DIV");

            //Set attributes
            overlay.classList.add("vModal", "xScroll");
            overlay.setAttribute("data-id", "vModalStyles");

            if (overlayBackgroundType == "color") {
                if (overlayStyle != "") overlay["style"] = overlayStyle;
            }

NEW_SECTION
        if ($$.ss("div[data-id='vModalStyles']") == null) {
            //Create
            var overlay = $$.ce("DIV");
            var effectsCon = $$.ce("DIV");

            //Set attributes
            overlay.classList.add("vModal", "xScroll");
            overlay.setAttribute("data-id", "vModalStyles");

            if (overlayBackgroundType == "color") {
                if (overlayStyle != "") overlay.style["background"] = overlayStyle;
            }

REASON
Two defects in one block. (a) The guard against building a second overlay searched
for `style[data-id='vModalStyles']`, but the marker is written onto the overlay
`<div>`, never onto a `<style>` element — so the guard never matched. A second
`ModalDisplayer` instance on the same page appended a second `.vModal` overlay, and
from then on the `$$.ss(".vModal")` lookups used throughout the module resolved to
whichever came first, breaking both instances. The selector now targets the `div`
that actually carries the marker.
(b) `overlay["style"] = overlayStyle` assigns to the element's `style` property,
which is a read-only `CSSStyleDeclaration`; the assignment was silently discarded
and `config.overlayStyle` had no effect at all — the overlay was always fully
transparent. The value is now applied as the `background` declaration, which accepts
the documented colour syntax. Verified by the "second instance does not duplicate
overlay" and "overlayStyle color applied to overlay" checks.

================================================================================
CHANGE 8 — Measure the modal once instead of twice
================================================================================

OLD_SECTION
        sY = scrollY;
        currentForm = modal;
        modal.id != null ? id = modal.id : null;
        computedModalHeight = getDimensionOfHidden(modal)["height"];
        computedModalWidth = getDimensionOfHidden(modal)["width"];
        cssWidth = $$.sm(modal).cssStyle("width");

NEW_SECTION
        sY = scrollY;
        currentForm = modal;
        modal.id != null ? id = modal.id : null;
        var modalDimension = getDimensionOfHidden(modal);
        computedModalHeight = modalDimension["height"];
        computedModalWidth = modalDimension["width"];
        cssWidth = $$.sm(modal).cssStyle("width");

REASON
`getDimensionOfHidden()` (src/helpers.js) is expensive and destructive: it mutates
the element's position/opacity/display, reads `scrollHeight` and `scrollWidth` —
each a forced synchronous layout — then restores the styles. Calling it twice to
read two members of the same returned object doubled that work and forced two extra
reflows on every open, for identical results. It is now called once and both
dimensions are read from the single result.

